\section{Generalization and Robustness}
\label{sec:generalization}

We test whether atlas accuracy (68.9--85.9\% four-class; d1--d3: 58.9--81.5\%) reflects genuine statistical regularities across eight generalization axes (five below, three in Appendix).

\subsection{Classifier Comparison and Surface-Text Ablation}
\label{sec:gen:baselines}

Table~\ref{tab:baselines} compares methods across all 9 cells.
Four classifiers (RF, XGBoost, ordinal MLP, prototypical) converge within 0.8\,pp under cross-model transfer (Appendix~\ref{app:learned_estimator}), confirming a feature-determined accuracy ceiling.

\paragraph{Neural end-to-end baselines.}
DeBERTa-v3-base (LoRA, 53--61\%) and RoBERTa-DCOL (ordinal contrastive, 64.5\%) lag RF by 14--33\,pp; note that RF features access reference-model log-probabilities unavailable to end-to-end PLM baselines (\S\ref{sec:discussion}).

\paragraph{Binary detectors and membership inference.}
Perturbation-based (DetectGPT, 28.8\%) and membership-inference (Min-K\% Prob, 33.2\%) methods produce scores with no ordinal resolution (Table~\ref{tab:baselines}); Binoculars d1v2 AUC\,=\,0.50--0.53 vs.\ RF 0.93 (Appendix~\ref{app:detector_baselines}). Binoculars convergence with our pipeline is discussed in \S\ref{sec:discussion}.

\paragraph{Surface-text ablation.}
Single-feature regression on mean perplexity (25.1\%) and zero-shot LLMs (best 36.2\%; Appendix~\ref{app:zeroshot_prompt}) confirm depth signal is inaccessible from surface text.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Method comparison (RF mean across 9 cells, 5-fold CV). Chance = 25\% / 33.3\% (d1--d3). $^\dagger$Adapted from binary detection; Binoculars 11-feat: $p{>}0.05$ vs.\ 19-feat RF (paired permutation test).}
\label{tab:baselines}
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{%
\begin{tabular}{@{}llcc@{}}
\toprule
Method & Calibration & 4-Cl (\%) & d1--d3 (\%) \\
\midrule
RF 19-feature (ours) & RF & 78.4 & \textbf{71.6} \\
Binoculars$^\dagger$ \cite{binoculars2024} & RF (11 feat) & 78.1 & --- \\
Merged (30 feat)$^\dagger$ & RF & \textbf{82.6} & --- \\
Binoculars \cite{binoculars2024} & threshold & 69.8 & --- \\
Binoculars \cite{binoculars2024} & RF (1 feat) & 63.7 & --- \\
RoBERTa-DCOL (ordinal) & contrastive & 64.5 & --- \\
DeBERTa-v3 (end-to-end) & cross-entropy & 53.1--60.6 & --- \\
\midrule
Zero-shot LLM (best) & --- & 36.2 & --- \\
Min-K\% Prob \cite{shi2024_minkprob} & CORAL & 33.2 & 40.4 \\
DetectGPT \cite{mitchell2023_detectgpt} & best-of-3 & 28.8 & 24.3 \\
Random & --- & 25.0 & 33.3 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\end{table}

\subsection{Cross-Model Transfer}
\label{sec:gen:crossmodel}

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Cross-model transfer (leave-one-out, nucleus only). Chance = 25\% / 33\% (d1--d3). d1--d3 mean 55.4\%; QWK\,=\,0.81; 94.8\% errors at $|\Delta| \leq 1$.}
\label{tab:cross_model}
\begin{tabular}{lccccc}
\toprule
Holdout & Acc & d0 & d1 & d2 & d3 \\
 & (\%) & Rec. & Rec. & Rec. & Rec. \\
\midrule
Pythia & 62.4 & 100.0 & 49.3 & 26.8 & 73.6 \\
OLMo & \textbf{69.2} & 99.3 & 75.5 & 43.3 & 58.8 \\
GPT-2 XL & 67.6 & 98.5 & 74.7 & 52.6 & 44.5 \\
\midrule
Mean & 66.4 & 99.3 & 66.5 & 40.9 & 59.0 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Mean transfer accuracy is 66.4\% ($2.7{\times}$ chance; Table~\ref{tab:cross_model}; d1--d3: 55.4\%); d2 worst case 26.8\% (Pythia holdout; confusion matrices in Figure~\ref{fig:confusion_matrix}).

\subsection{Cross-Strategy Transfer}
\label{sec:gen:crossstrategy}

Cross-strategy transfer reveals a structural barrier: d1--d3 balanced accuracy drops to 45.9\% (four-class: 59.1\%), 7\,pp below cross-model transfer (Table~\ref{tab:cross_strategy}).
A two-stage pipeline (strategy classifier $\to$ depth classifier) recovers much of this gap (70.5\%, $+$11.5\,pp; oracle: 78.3\%; Appendix~\ref{app:extended_discussion}).
Our depth estimation claims hold most strongly within-strategy or when the decoding configuration is known.

%% ----------------------------------------------------------------
\subsection{Preliminary Cross-Scale Observations (14B)}
\label{sec:gen:crossscale}

At 14B (Qwen2.5-14B), self-reference yields 50.8\% ($2{\times}$ chance, $\mathrm{EDD}_{0.70}{=}0$); switching to cross-reference (Pythia-1.4B) shifts accuracy by $-$6.8\,pp to 44.1\% but raises $\mathrm{EDD}_{0.70}$ to 2 for nucleus and temperature, indicating that reference-model choice is a major confound in cross-scale comparisons (Appendix~\ref{app:cross_scale_full}).
Cross-domain transfer to arXiv abstracts reaches 74.1\% (Appendix~\ref{app:domain_bias}).

